I'll give you the Jinja for this order-shipped email. Since this is **transactional** (about a specific order), missing values should suppress the send rather than falling back.

## Subject line

```jinja
{{UserAttribute['First Name']|default('there', true)}}, your order {{EventAttribute['Order ID']}} has shipped
```

## Body

**Guards first** — these prevent sending when critical data is missing:

```jinja
{% if not EventAttribute['Order ID'] %}
  {% MOE_NOT_SEND("Order ID missing on Order Shipped event") %}
{% endif %}
{% if not EventAttribute['Tracking URL'] %}
  {% MOE_NOT_SEND("Tracking URL missing on Order Shipped event") %}
{% endif %}
{% if not EventAttribute['Delivery Date'] %}
  {% MOE_NOT_SEND("Delivery Date missing on Order Shipped event") %}
{% endif %}
```

**Then the content:**

```jinja
<p>Your order {{EventAttribute['Order ID']|e}} has shipped!</p>

<p><a href="{{EventAttribute['Tracking URL']|e}}">Track your order</a></p>

<p>Estimated delivery: {{EventAttribute['Delivery Date']|dateFormatter('%d %b %Y')}}</p>
```

## Critical notes

**This requires an event-triggered campaign.** EventAttribute only exists there — in a one-time or periodic campaign, every value would be null and every recipient would be dropped.

**Attribute names must match exactly.** Replace `Order ID`, `Tracking URL`, and `Delivery Date` with the exact readable names from your Order Shipped event (case- and space-sensitive). Use the `@` picker in MoEngage rather than typing them.

**The tracking URL guard is not optional.** MoEngage has *"no fallback mechanism for personalized URLs"* — if the attribute in the link doesn't resolve, the email silently doesn't send. The separate `{% MOE_NOT_SEND() %}` guard is the only way that drop gets labelled in your Error breakdown.

**Autoescape is off in MoEngage.** The `|e` filters are there because event data is untrusted — a malformed order ID or URL could break the layout or worse. Validate that Tracking URL only contains HTTPS URLs from your expected shipping-provider domains.

**Date format assumes parseable input.** `dateFormatter` will reformat the date, but if Delivery Date arrives in an unexpected format, test it in preview first. The `%d %b %Y` gives you "12 Mar 2026".

